('acid test','/ˌæsɪd ˈtɛst/','rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A test involving the reaction of an acid with another chemical.','noun','From acid + test; sense 1 (“test involving the reaction of an acid with another chemical”) and sense 2 (“rigorous test or appraisal of the quality or worth of something”) refer to the use of nitric acid to test if a metal is gold; true gold is not affected by the acid.
Sense 3 (“party based around the use and advocacy of the psychedelic drug LSD”) is a pun referencing the slang term acid (“LSD”). The original “acid test” parties were thrown by the Merry Pranksters group between 1965 and 1966 and advertised by the slogan “Can you pass the acid test?”',555,'200plus',1,0.65,17),
('Whobrey',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','Of uncertain origin, but perhaps related to the English toponym Whoberley.',74,'40_80',1,0.4,18),
('bifid',NULL,'uncommon',2.25,'plain','["adjective"]','Cleft; divided into two principal or main parts, such as two lobes.','adjective','From Latin bifidus (“divided in two”), from bi- (“two”) + -fidus, combining form of findere (“to split”).',105,'80_120',1,0.6,19),
