('array','/əˈɹeɪ/','common',4.5,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','Clothing and ornamentation; raiment.','noun','From Middle English arrayen, from Anglo-Norman arraier (compare Old French arraier, areer (“to put in order”)), from Vulgar Latin *arrēdō (“to put in order, arrange, array”), from *rēdum (“preparation, order”), from Frankish *raid or *raidā (“preparation, order”) or Gothic 𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃 (garaiþs, “ready, prepared”), from Proto-Germanic *raidaz, *raidiz (“ready”). Compare Old English rād (“condition, stipulation”), Old High German antreitī (“order, rank”). Doublet of ready.',471,'200plus',1,0.8,41),
('Staffordshire','/ˈstæf.əd.ʃə(ɹ)/','common',3.33,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A ceremonial county of West Midlands, England, bordered by Shropshire, Cheshire, Derbyshire, Warwickshire, West Midlands and Worcestershire.','proper noun','From Middle English Staffordschire, from late Old English Stæffordsċīr; equivalent to Stafford + -shire.',104,'80_120',1,0.65,42),
