('chart','/t͡ʃɑɹt/','common',4.55,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A map illustrating the geography of a specific phenomenon.','noun','Borrowed from Middle French charte (“card, map”), from Late Latin charta (“paper, card, map”), Latin charta (“papyrus, writing”), from Ancient Greek χάρτης (khártēs, “papyrus, thin sheet”). Doublet of card and carte; related to charter.',236,'200plus',1,0.8,72),
('hundredman',NULL,'rare',0.45,'plain','["noun"]','A centurion.','noun','From Old English hundredmann, equivalent to hundred + -man.',59,'40_80',1,0.45,73),
('wisdom','/ˈwɪzdəm/','common',4.46,'plain','["noun"]','An element of personal character that enables one to distinguish the wise from the unwise.','noun','From Middle English wisdom, from Old English wīsdōm (“wisdom”), from Proto-West Germanic *wīsadōm, from Proto-Germanic *wīsadōmaz (“wisdom”), corresponding to wise + -dom. Cognate with Scots wisdom, wysdom (“wisdom”), West Frisian wiisdom (“wisdom”), Dutch wijsdom (“wisdom”), German Weistum (“legal sentence”), Danish/Norwegian/Swedish visdom (“wisdom”), Icelandic vísdómur (“wisdom”).',386,'200plus',1,0.8,74),
('Darlingtonia',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','An unincorporated community in Del Norte County, California, United States.','proper noun','Named after Darlingtonia californica, common in the region, from Darlington + Latin -ia.',88,'80_120',1,0.54,75),
('devoid','/dɪˈvɔɪd/','common',3.61,'plain','["verb", "adjective"]','Completely without; having none of','adjective','From obsolete Middle English verb devoiden, from Old French desvuidier (“to empty out”) (compare French dévider).',113,'80_120',1,0.7,76),
('lay pipe',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["verb"]','Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see lay, pipe.','verb','The figurative sense is a jocular extension from the literal sense referring to the work of a pipelayer (a tradesperson).',121,'120_200',0,0.6,77),
('Clymene','/ˈklɪmɪni/','scarce',1.15,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A nymph married to Iapetus, mother of Prometheus, Epimetheus, Menoetius and Atlas.','proper noun','Borrowed from Ancient Greek Κλυμένη (Kluménē).',46,'40_80',1,0.49,78),
('riot stairs',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A flight of stairs that is constructed to deliberately disrupt the natural strides of people walking or running on them.','noun','Coined for the construction of such stairways on university campuses in response to student unrest during the 1960s and 1970s in an effort to prevent mobs of students from running together.',189,'120_200',0,0.64,79),
('garland','/ˈɡɑɹ.lənd/','common',3.63,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A circular or linear decoration, especially one of plaited flowers or leaves, worn on the body or draped as a decoration.','noun','From Middle English garland, garlaunde, gerland, from Old French garlande, garlaunde, gerlande, guerlande (compare French guirlande), from Frankish *wierlōn, *wieralōn, a frequentative form of Frankish *wierōn (“to adorn, bedeck”), from *wiera (“a gold thread”), from or related to Proto-Germanic *wīraz.
Akin to Old High German wieren (“to adorn”), Old High German wiara (“gold thread”). More at wire.',402,'200plus',1,0.8,80),
('pollution','/pəˈluʃən/','common',4.46,'plain','["noun"]','Physical contamination, now especially the contamination of the environment by harmful substances, or by disruptive levels of noise, light etc.','noun','From Middle English pollucioun, pollucion (“desecration, impurity”), from Anglo-Norman pollutiun, Middle French pollution, pollucion, and their source, post-classical Latin pollūtiō (“defilement, desecration; nocturnal emission”) (4th century), from the participial stem of polluō (“to soil, defile, contaminate”), from por- (“before”) + -luō (“to smear”), related to lutum (“mud”) and luēs (“filth”). Compare Ancient Greek λῦμα (lûma, “filth, dirt, disgrace”) and λῦμαξ (lûmax, “rubbish, refuse”), Old Irish loth (“mud, dirt”), Lithuanian lutynas (“pool, puddle”). By surface analysis, pollute + -ion.',602,'200plus',1,0.8,81),
('peltast',NULL,'scarce',1.44,'plain','["noun"]','A type of light infantry unit in Ancient Greece who often served as skirmishers.','noun','From Ancient Greek πελταστής (peltastḗs).',41,'40_80',1,0.45,82),
('abdabs','/ˈæb.dæbz/','rare',0.18,'plain','["noun"]','Extreme nervousness or anxiety; terror; heebie-jeebies.','noun','Unknown. Possibly imitative of the spluttering speech of one who is extremely nervous or anxious.',97,'80_120',0,0.5,83),
('Colombier paper',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A large size of paper for drawings.','noun','French',6,'lt40',1,0.3,84),
('silicon','/ˈsɪlɪkən/','common',4.09,'plain','["noun"]','A nonmetallic element (symbol Si) with an atomic number of 14 and atomic weight of 28.0855.','noun','Coined by Scottish chemist Thomas Thomson as a modification of the earlier name silicium, from the stem of Latin silex (“flint, silica”) + -on from carbon.',155,'120_200',1,0.74,85),
('Yamaguchi','/jɑməˈɡut͡ʃi/','uncommon',2.62,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A city in Japan.','proper noun','Borrowed from Japanese 山口(やまぐち) (Yamaguchi).',44,'40_80',1,0.54,86),
('ach-laut','/ˈɑːx laʊt/','rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["noun"]','The guttural phoneme /x/ (the voiceless velar fricative) of the German language, the sound of the letters ch in German coming immediately after a, o and u (central and back vowels), also found in other languages.','noun','From German Ach-Laut, ach-Laut, from ach (which has this sound) + Laut (“sound”).',81,'80_120',1,0.55,87),
('Albarian',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Armenian.','proper noun','From Armenian Ալբարյան (Albaryan).',34,'lt40',1,0.3,88),
('wontsome',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["adjective"]','Characterised or marked by wont; habitual, customary, usual.','adjective','From wont + -some.
Compare Middle English wontsumnes (“custom, habit”, literally “wontsomeness”).',97,'80_120',1,0.54,89),
('Micawber','/mɪˈkɒbər/','scarce',1.78,'capitalized','["noun", "verb"]','A person who is poor but eternally optimistic, believing that "something will turn up", like the fictional character Wilkins Micawber in the 1850 Charles Dickens novel David Copperfield.','noun','A name likely coined by Charles Dickens, evidently reminiscent of common Irish, Scots, and Scottish Gaelic surnames starting with Mc-, Mac-, both from Old Irish macc (“son, child”) (see more there). For the second particle, compare names like Aubrey; similar surnames like M(a)cAubrey are attested around Dickens'' time and thus Micawber may represent a dialectal variation.',373,'200plus',1,0.8,90),
('Gârbova',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized,nonascii','["proper noun"]','A village and commune of Alba County, Romania.','proper noun','Unadapted borrowing from Romanian Gârbova.',42,'40_80',1,0.44,91),
('beadle','/ˈbiːdəl/','uncommon',2.66,'plain','["noun"]','A parish constable, a uniformed minor (lay) official, who ushers and keeps order.','noun','From Middle English bedel, bidel, from Old English bydel (“warrant officer, apparitor”), from Proto-West Germanic *budil, from Proto-Germanic *budilaz (“herald”), equivalent to bid + -le. Cognate with Dutch beul, German Büttel. More at bid.',240,'200plus',1,0.8,92),
('stathmograph',NULL,'unattested',-2.19,'plain','["noun"]','A device for recording the speed of a railway train.','noun','From Ancient Greek στάθμη (státhmē, “a measuring line”) + -graph.',65,'40_80',1,0.4,93),
('levee','/ˈlɛvi/','common',3.36,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','An elevated ridge of deposited sediment on the banks of a river, formed by the river''s overflow at times of high discharge.','noun','From French levé variant of the noun lever (“the act of getting up in the morning”).',84,'80_120',1,0.6,94),
('verdour',NULL,'obscure',-0.29,'plain','["noun"]','Verdure.','noun','From Middle English verdour, a variation of verdure, from Middle French verdour, verdure.',89,'80_120',1,0.6,95),
('glycosaminoglycan',NULL,'uncommon',2.46,'plain','["noun"]','Any polysaccharide that is a polymer of amino sugars; they are the carbohydrate units of proteoglycans.','noun','From glycosamino- (“combining form of glycosamine”) + glycan (“polysaccharide”); compare aminoglycan.',101,'80_120',1,0.55,96),
('Yousaf',NULL,'uncommon',2.39,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Arabic, equivalent to English Joseph.','proper noun','Variant of Youssef. From Urdu يوسف, from Arabic يُوسِف (yūsif), from Hebrew יוסף. This variant is mostly found in Pakistan.',123,'120_200',1,0.8,97),
('Berca',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A commune of Buzău County, Romania.','proper noun','Unadapted borrowing from Romanian Berca.',40,'40_80',1,0.44,98),
('trillo',NULL,'scarce',1.8,'plain','["noun"]','A trill or shake.','noun','Borrowed from Italian trillo.',29,'lt40',1,0.39,99),
('Hartsough',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from German.','proper noun','Americanized form of German Herzog.',35,'lt40',1,0.3,100);
INSERT INTO word (word,ipa,tier,zipf,shape,pos,definition,def_pos,etymology,etym_len,etym_band,has_signal,prior,shuffle) VALUES
('Cumaean','/kjuːˈmiː.ən/','rare',NULL,'capitalized','["noun", "adjective"]','A native or inhabitant of Cumae','noun','From Latin Cumaeus + -an. By surface analysis, Cum(ae) + -aean.',63,'40_80',1,0.45,101),
('quinquefoil','/ˈkwɪŋkwɪfɔɪl/','marginal',-1.1,'pointer_only','["noun"]','Alternative form of cinquefoil.','noun','Alteration of cinquefoil after the original Latin etymon quīnquefolium.',71,'40_80',1,0.4,102),
('lairdship',NULL,'rare',0.76,'plain','["noun"]','The state or condition of being a laird.','noun','Borrowed from Middle Scots lairdschip (modern Scots lairdship), from Early Scots lardschip, from Old English hlāfordsċipe; by surface analysis, laird + -ship and a doublet of lordship.',184,'120_200',1,0.78,103),
('637',NULL,'common',3.23,'digits','["noun", "proper noun", "phrase"]','A submarine of the Sturgeon class','noun','From the hull number of the first boat of the class, USS Sturgeon (SSN-637), Sturgeon-class nuclear-powered attack submarine',124,'120_200',0,0.65,104),
('Leeuwarderadeel',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A former municipality of Friesland, Netherlands.','proper noun','Borrowed from Dutch Leeuwarderadeel, from West Frisian Ljouwerteradiel.',71,'40_80',1,0.54,105),
('veldtschoon',NULL,'obscure',-0.38,'plain','["noun"]','A shoe of untanned leather.','noun','From Afrikaans.',15,'lt40',1,0.35,106),
('Port aux Basques','/ˈpɔɹt oʊ ˈbæsk/','rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','A village and port in Channel-Port aux Basques, Newfoundland, Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada.','proper noun','From French Port aux Basques, derived in turn from port (“port”) and the plural form of basque (“Basque”). From being a haven used by Basque whalers in the 16th century, later becoming a settlement in the 17th century.',218,'200plus',1,0.7,107),
('Yunus',NULL,'uncommon',2.5,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','Jonah, a major prophet of Islam.','proper noun','Borrowed from Arabic يُونُس (yūnus), from Ancient Greek Ἰωνᾶς (Iōnâs), from Hebrew יונה (yônā), all meaning Jonah. Doublet of Younis and Younus.',144,'120_200',1,0.8,108),
('parallactic',NULL,'scarce',1.29,'plain','["adjective"]','Of or pertaining to parallax','adjective','From New Latin parallacticus, from Ancient Greek παραλλακτικός (parallaktikós).',79,'40_80',1,0.5,109),
('thole','/θoʊl/','scarce',1.96,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A pin in the side of a boat which acts as a fulcrum for the oars.','noun','From Middle English thō̆le (“a peg”), from Old English þol, þoll (“oar-pin, rowlock; thole”), from Proto-West Germanic *þoll, from Proto-Germanic *þullaz, *þullō (“beam; thole”), from Proto-Indo-European *tūl-, *twel- (“bush; sphere”). The word is cognate with Danish told (“thole”), Dutch dol (“thole; oarlock”), Low German Doll (“thole; oarlock”).',349,'200plus',1,0.8,110),
('béarnaise sauce',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,nonascii','["noun"]','A sauce made of clarified butter emulsified in egg yolks and white wine vinegar and flavored with herbs.','noun','from French, the feminine of béarnais (the adjective from the Béarn region, using -ais (“-ese”)) + sauce',104,'80_120',1,0.55,111),
('Yatsko',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Russian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Russian Яцко (Jacko).',35,'lt40',1,0.34,112),
('Codrington',NULL,'scarce',1.86,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A hamlet in Dodington parish, South Gloucestershire district, Gloucestershire, England (OS grid ref ST726617).','proper noun','From the Old English personal name Cūþhere + -ing (“belonging to”) + tūn (“enclosure; settlement, town”).',105,'80_120',1,0.55,113),
('seine','/seɪn/','common',3.39,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A long net having floats attached at the top and sinkers (weights) at the bottom, used in shallow water for catching fish.','noun','From Old English seġne, from Proto-West Germanic *sagīna, from Latin sagēna, from Ancient Greek σαγήνη (sagḗnē, “dragnet”), of unknown origin.',142,'120_200',1,0.8,114),
('lyophilization',NULL,'scarce',1.94,'pointer_only','["noun"]','Synonym of freeze-drying.','noun','First use appears c. 1894 in the US. lyo- + philos + -ization (alternatively analyzed as lyophilize + -ation), from Ancient Greek λύω (lúō, “to loosen, to dissolve”) + φίλος (phílos, “beloved”) + -ίζειν (-ízein) + Latin -ātiō, due to the dried product being able to rapidly reabsorb the solvent and restore the original substance, hence “lyophil” (solvent-loving). Popularized from 1960 by Louis R. Rey. Cognates exist in other European languages by means of the same construction.',481,'200plus',1,0.79,115),
('small intestine',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','The upper part of the intestine, between the stomach and the large intestine, divided into the duodenum, the jejunum and the ileum.','noun','From small + intestine, perhaps a partial calque of Middle English smaltharm, smaleþarm, from Old English smælþearme (“lower gut, small intestine”, literally “small tharm”).',173,'120_200',1,0.74,116),
('trident','/ˈtɹaɪ̯dənt/','common',3.27,'plain','["noun"]','A three-pronged spear somewhat resembling a pitchfork.','noun','PIE word
 *tréyes
From Latin tridēns, from tri- (“three”) + dēns (“tooth”).',75,'40_80',1,0.5,117),
('pollinate','/ˈpɒlɪneɪt/','uncommon',2.9,'plain','["verb", "adjective"]','To apply pollen to (a stigma).','verb','From pollin- (compound form of "pollen") + -ate (verb-forming suffix). From oblique stem of Latin pollen, pollinis.',115,'80_120',1,0.6,118),
('castellar',NULL,'scarce',1.1,'plain','["adjective"]','Somewhat like a castle.','adjective','From Latin castellum + -ar. By surface analysis, castle + -ar.',62,'40_80',1,0.45,119),
('classis','/ˈklæsɪs/','common',4.77,'plain','["noun"]','An ecclesiastical body or judicatory in certain churches, such as the Dutch Reformed Church. It is intermediate between the consistory and the synod, and corresponds to the presbytery in the Presbyterian church.','noun','Borrowed from Latin classis. Doublet of class.',46,'40_80',1,0.58,120),
('urbs','/ɝbz/','uncommon',2.02,'plain','["noun"]','A walled city in Ancient Rome.','noun','Borrowed from Latin urbs.',25,'lt40',1,0.44,121),
('coarsely','/ˈkɔː(ɹ)sli/','common',3.01,'plain','["adverb"]','in a coarse manner','adverb','From earlier coursely, from Middle English coursly (“naturally, logically”); equivalent to coarse + -ly.',104,'80_120',1,0.6,122),
('imploratory',NULL,'obscure',-0.73,'plain','["adjective"]','Entreating, supplicatory.','adjective','From Latin implōrātus + English -atory (suffix forming adjectives of, relating to, or connected with [the specified thing]). Implōrātus is the perfect passive participle of implōrō (“to beseech, entreat, implore; to appeal to, pray to”): see further at implore.',261,'200plus',1,0.75,123),
('wrenchful',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["adjective"]','Causing great pain, suffering, or agony; painful','adjective','From wrench (verb) + -ful. Compare Middle English wrenchful (“crafty, deceitful”).',82,'80_120',1,0.5,124),
('Burgenland','/ˈbɜː(ɹ)ɡənlænd/','scarce',1.47,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','The easternmost and least populous federal state of Austria.','proper noun','From the German Burgenland. Literally, “land of castles”.',57,'40_80',1,0.49,125),
('Battaglia',NULL,'uncommon',2.16,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Italian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Battaglia. Doublet of Battle.',51,'40_80',1,0.58,126),
('antonym','/ˈæn.təˌnɪm/','uncommon',2.9,'plain','["noun"]','A word which has the opposite meaning of another word.','noun','From French antonyme (1840s and 1850s), which was modeled on earlier synonyme and influenced by the etymons of Ancient Greek ἀντωνυμία (antōnumía, “pronoun”); credit for popularization of the French loanword''s naturalization into English is given principally to Charles John Smith and his 1867 book Synonyms and Antonyms: Or, Kindred Words and Their Opposites. Collected and Contrasted. By surface analysis, ant- + -onym.',421,'200plus',1,0.8,127),
('Kusiak',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Polish.','proper noun','Borrowed from Polish Kusiak.',28,'lt40',1,0.34,128),
('samsonite',NULL,'uncommon',2.15,'plain','["noun"]','A black, monoclinic silver manganese antimony sulfosalt mineral.','noun','From Samson + -ite, after an occurrence in the Samson Vein of the Sankt Andreasberg silver mines, Harz Mountains, Germany.',122,'120_200',0,0.65,129),
('pneumology','/n(j)ʊˈmɒləd͡ʒi/','scarce',1.14,'plain','["noun"]','The study of the respiratory system and organs.','noun','From New Latin pneumologia, from Ancient Greek πνεύμων (pneúmōn, “lung”) + -logia (“-logy”). By surface analysis, pneumo- + -logy.',130,'120_200',1,0.7,130),
('repertitious',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["adjective"]','Found by chance.','adjective','From Latin repertīcius (“newly found”), from repertus (“found, discovered, invented”), from reperīre (“to find, discover, invent”), from re- (“again, anew”) + parere (“to bear, to get”), + -īcius (“-itious”), under influence of English -ious, q.v. Cognate with repertible and repertor.',285,'200plus',1,0.74,131),
('Huainanzi',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A 2nd-century bc record of Chinese mythology, legend, history, and thought.','proper noun','From the Hanyu Pinyin romanization of Mandarin 淮南子 (Huáinánzǐ, “Writings of the Masters South of the Huai River”), the usual Chinese name of the work, from its origin among the scholars at the court of Liu An, prince of Huainan.',228,'200plus',1,0.7,132),
('tweenlight',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["noun"]','The period between dusk and nightfall; twilight.','noun','From Old English twēonlēoht (“twilight”, literally “double-/doubtful-/uncertain-light”). The alternative spellings are due to a misassociation with between, erroneously interpreting tweenlight as if it were a shortening of between-light.',237,'200plus',1,0.64,133),
('erhu','/ˈɜːhuː/','scarce',1.5,'plain','["noun"]','A type of bowed spike fiddle having two strings, originating in China as part of the huqin family of string instruments.','noun','From Mandarin 二胡 (èrhú), from 二 (èr, “two”) + 胡 (hú, “fiddle; short for 胡琴, a family of Chinese vertical fiddles”).',115,'80_120',1,0.6,134),
('Site Q',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','A formerly unidentified archeological site of a Mayan city, identified in the late 1990s and early 2000s as La Corona.','proper noun','Coined by Peter Mathews, referring to Spanish ¿qué?.',52,'40_80',1,0.44,135),
('Krusec',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Slovene.','proper noun','From Slovene Krušec. Compare krušec (“little piece of bread”), hruška (“pear”).',79,'40_80',1,0.35,136),
('Aarne-Thompson-Uther index',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,hyphenated','["proper noun"]','A catalogue of traditional tales based around motifs used in folklore studies.','proper noun','After Finnish folklorist Antti Aarne, who originally devised the index, and its elaborators American folklorist Stith Thompson and German folklorist Hans-Jörg Uther.',165,'120_200',1,0.69,137),
('dipso-',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'hyphenated','["prefix"]','thirst; drinking','prefix','From Ancient Greek δίψα (dípsa, “thirst”).',42,'40_80',1,0.4,138),
('baptisia',NULL,'scarce',1.35,'plain','["noun"]','Any of various ornamental plants of the genus Baptisia.','noun','Learned borrowing from taxonomic name Baptisia, derived from Ancient Greek βαπτίζω (baptízō, “to immerse”), in reference to the plant''s use as a source of dye.',159,'120_200',1,0.69,139),
('topiary','/ˈtoʊpiˌɛəɹi/','uncommon',2.3,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','Art or practice of trimming shrubs or trees in artistic or ornamental shapes, e.g. of animals.','noun','From Latin topiarius (“of or relating to ornamental gardening; an ornamental garden, an ornamental gardener”), from Latin topia (“ornamental gardening, landscape painting”), from Ancient Greek τόπια (tópia, “artistic representation in which natural or artificial features of a place are used as the medium”), plural of Ancient Greek τόπιον (tópion, “field, landscape”), from τόπος (tópos, “place”). The adjective use dates to 1592, the noun use dates to 1908.',459,'200plus',1,0.8,140),
('Ince','/ˈɪns/','uncommon',2.73,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','From Proto-Brythonic *ɨnɨs (“island”) or else the Cumbric/Old Welsh reflex inis. Compare Ynys and Inch.',103,'80_120',1,0.65,141),
('Exmouth','/ˈɛksməθ/','uncommon',2.34,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A town and civil parish with a town council in East Devon district, Devon, England, at the mouth of the River Exe (OS grid ref SY0081).','proper noun','In England, from Old English Exanmūþa recorded c. 1025, equivalent to Exe + mouth.',82,'80_120',1,0.6,142),
('volant','/ˈvoʊlənt/','scarce',1.8,'plain','["adjective"]','Having extended wings as if flying.','adjective','From Middle French volant, present participle of voler (“to fly”), from Latin volāre.',85,'80_120',1,0.6,143),
('chromule',NULL,'marginal',-1.31,'plain','["noun"]','Any colouring matter in plants other than chlorophyll, and especially in the petals.','noun','From Ancient Greek χρῶμα (khrôma, “colour”) and ὕλη (húlē, “matter”).',69,'40_80',1,0.4,144),
('nucule',NULL,'rare',0.1,'plain','["noun"]','The oogonium of a charophyte.','noun','Borrowed from Latin nucula (“little nut”).',42,'40_80',1,0.49,145),
('Sink',NULL,'common',4.21,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','From the Dutch and northern German surname, from Zink (“cornetto, trumpet-like instrument”).',92,'80_120',1,0.65,146),
('Lopresti',NULL,'scarce',1.69,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Italian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Lopresti.',31,'lt40',1,0.39,147),
('Conway','/ˈkɒnweɪ/','common',3.61,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','The River Conway, the former English name of the River Conwy in North Wales.','proper noun','Anglicization of Welsh Conwy',28,'lt40',1,0.4,148),
('Stephensen',NULL,'scarce',1.49,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Danish or Norwegian.','proper noun','From Danish or Norwegian Stephensen, patronymic from Stephen + -sen.',68,'40_80',1,0.5,149),
('Rega',NULL,'uncommon',2.15,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Italian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Rega.',27,'lt40',1,0.44,150),
('brunaille',NULL,'obscure',-0.91,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A method of painting or other work that uses shades of brown primarily or exclusively.','noun','From French brunaille, from brun (“brown”). Compare grisaille.',62,'40_80',1,0.45,151),
('Galentine''s Day',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','An American cultural holiday on that celebrates women''s friendship, around the time of Saint Valentine''s Day, typically on February 13.','proper noun','Blend of gal + Valentine''s Day, originally the title and subject of the 16th episode of the second season of the American comedy television series Parks and Recreation, first broadcast in 2010.',193,'120_200',0,0.64,152),
('Kubachi ware',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A style of Persian fritware pottery, believed to have been created during the Safavid Period in the northwestern part of what is now Iran.','noun','Named after the town of Kubachi in Dagestan, modern-day Russia, though that place is no longer believed to be the origin.',121,'120_200',0,0.59,153),
('trove','/tɹoʊv/','common',3.04,'plain','["noun"]','A treasure trove; a collection of treasure.','noun','Originally in the phrase treasure trove, from Anglo-Norman tresor trouvé (“found treasure”), where the past participle trouvé (“found”) was interpreted in English as a noun. Doublet of trope.',191,'120_200',1,0.8,154),
('Chun''an',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','A county of Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China.','proper noun','From the Hanyu Pinyin romanization of the Mandarin 淳安 (Chún''ān).',64,'40_80',1,0.45,155),
('Pei',NULL,'common',3.0,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','Borrowed from Mandarin 裴 (Péi) or 貝／贝 (Bèi) (Wade–Giles romanisation: Pei⁴).',76,'40_80',1,0.54,156),
('in a while, crocodile',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword','["phrase"]','Au revoir, see you soon.','phrase','Modification of a lyric from the song See You Later, Alligator (1955), chosen for the rhyme.',92,'80_120',0,0.45,157),
('Beja',NULL,'scarce',1.64,'capitalized','["noun", "adjective", "proper noun"]','An ethnic group found along the Horn of Africa.','noun','Borrowed from Arabic بَجَا (bajā), from Beja Bidhááwi.',54,'40_80',1,0.49,158),
('offhold',NULL,'marginal',-1.46,'plain','["verb"]','To hold off; prevent; refrain; avoid; shun.','verb','From Middle English ofholden, from Old English ofhealdan (“to withhold, retain”), equivalent to off- + hold. Cognate with Dutch afhouden (“to detain, deter”), German abhalten (“to discourage, prevent, deter”).',209,'200plus',1,0.79,159),
('flexor digitorum superficialis',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A muscle in the intermediate layer of the anterior compartment of the human forearm.','noun','From flexor (muscle that bends) + Latin digitorum (genitive singular; literally "finger") + Latin superficialis (relating to the surface), in all, "surface bending finger muscle".',179,'120_200',1,0.69,160),
('footle','/ˈfuːtəl/','scarce',1.42,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','nonsense; foolishness.','noun','Probably variant of footer (“to screw around”), from obsolete fouter (“an act of sexual intercourse”), from French foutre (“to have sexual intercourse”), from Latin futuere.',173,'120_200',1,0.7,161),
('graveyard shift',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A work shift late at night, especially one from midnight to 8 AM on a three-shift schedule.','noun','By association of the spooky, cold and silent properties of both the night and graveyards.',90,'80_120',0,0.5,162),
('décollement',NULL,'scarce',1.68,'nonascii','["noun"]','A gliding plane between two rock masses; a basal detachment fault.','noun','Borrowed from French décollement.',33,'lt40',1,0.39,163),
('sycamore','/ˈsɪkəmoɹ/','uncommon',2.94,'plain','["noun"]','Any of several North American plane trees, of the genus Platanus, especially Platanus occidentalis (American sycamore), distinguished by its mottled bark which flakes off in large irregular masses.','noun','Circa 1350 as Middle English sicamour, from Old French sicamor, from Latin sȳcomorus, from Ancient Greek σῡκόμορος (sūkómoros, literally “fig-mulberry”), from σῦκον (sûkon, “fig”) + μόρον (móron, “mulberry”). Possibly influenced by Hebrew שִׁקְמָה (shikmá, “fig-mulberry”).
In the 16th c. applied to the European maple (Acer pseudoplatanus), in the early 19th c. to various plane tree species introduced to North America, perhaps in analogy to their shadiness.',460,'200plus',1,0.8,164),
('serpiginous',NULL,'scarce',1.65,'plain','["adjective"]','creeping, advancing','adjective','Latin serpigio (“creeping and spreading skin disease”).',55,'40_80',1,0.45,165),
('Wik',NULL,'scarce',1.78,'capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','An indigenous Australian people from northern Queensland.','noun','Borrowed from Norwegian and Swedish Wik.',40,'40_80',1,0.54,166),
('Archimedes','/ˌɑːɹkɪˈmiːdiːz/','uncommon',2.69,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A transliteration of the Ancient Greek male given name Ἀρχιμήδης (Arkhimḗdēs), notably borne by Archimedes of Syracuse, an Ancient Greek mathematician, physicist and engineer (c. 287–212 BCE).','proper noun','Learned borrowing from Latin Archimēdēs, from Ancient Greek Ἀρχιμήδης (Arkhimḗdēs).',83,'80_120',1,0.69,167),
('Zhenbeipu',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A town in Xixia district, Yinchuan, Ningxia autonomous region, China.','proper noun','From the Hanyu Pinyin romanization of the Mandarin 鎮北堡 /镇北堡.',60,'40_80',1,0.45,168),
('moege',NULL,'obscure',-0.58,'plain','["noun"]','A kind of Japanese visual novel featuring moe content, including cute characters and art style.','noun','Borrowed from Japanese 萌(も)えゲー (moegē), a combination of 萌(も)え (moe, “moe”) and ゲー (gē), a shortening of ゲーム (gēmu, “game”), itself from English game.',150,'120_200',1,0.74,169),
('Ludești',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized,nonascii','["proper noun"]','A commune of Dâmbovița County, Romania.','proper noun','From Romanian Ludești.',22,'lt40',1,0.3,170),
('prvalue','/piˈɑɹ.væl.ju/','rare',0.25,'plain','["noun"]','An rvalue that is not an xvalue.','noun','Short for pure rvalue. According to Bjarne Stroustrup, coined by ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 22 in 2010.',93,'80_120',0,0.54,171),
('Lundmark',NULL,'scarce',1.25,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Swedish.','proper noun','Borrowed from Swedish Lundmark, from lund (“grove”) + mark (“ground, field”).',77,'40_80',1,0.49,172),
('neutral ground','/ˈnuːtɹəl ɡraʊnd/','rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','The median strip or central reservation between lanes of traffic in a divided highway.','noun','(Sense 1) Per Chase (1979), probably the first explanation of the term neutral ground is by George Cable in 1884: "The people of New Orleans take pride in Canal street^([sic])... Its two distinct granite paved roadways are each forty feet wide, and the tree-bordered ''neutral ground'' measures fifty-four feet across. It was ''neutral'' when it divided between the French Quarter [i.e., the Creole section] and the Americans [the Anglo section] at the time when their municipality governments were distinct from each other." The Creole and Anglo sections of the city were historically antagonistic toward one another, making the small space between them a notable, if somewhat jocular, "neutral" space.',699,'200plus',1,0.7,173),
('overbold',NULL,'scarce',1.13,'plain','["adjective"]','Too bold; impertinent or overreaching.','adjective','From Middle English overbold, equivalent to over- + bold.',57,'40_80',1,0.45,174),
('Japan','/d͡ʒəˈpæn/','common',4.89,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A country in East Asia. Capital and largest city: Tokyo.','proper noun','First attested in English as Giapan in Richard Willes''s 1577 The History of Travayle in the West and East Indies (cited in Peter C. Mancall''s Travel Narratives from the Age of Discovery, pp. 156–57), translating a 19 February 1565 letter of the Portuguese Jesuit missionary Luís Fróis as "Of the Ilande of Giapan".
Borrowed from Portuguese Japam /Japão with possible influence from Dutch Japan, both from Malay Jepang, from Hokkien 日本 (Ji̍t-pún), from Middle Chinese 日本 (nyit pwon^X, “sun origin”).
With /j/ readings, such as Iaponia /Japonia /Japon /Iapon from possibly Cantonese 日本 (jat⁶ bun²), also from Middle Chinese 日本 (nyit pwon^X, “sun origin”).
Compare also modern Mandarin 日本 (Rìběn), Japanese 日本(にっぽん) (Nippon) / 日本(にほん) (Nihon) (whence English doublets Nippon and Nihon), Korean 일본 (Ilbon) (日本), Vietnamese Nhật Bản (日本).
The earliest form of Japan in Europe was Marco Polo''s Cipangu, from some form of synonymous Sinitic 日本國 /日本国 (“Japan state”).',959,'200plus',1,0.8,175),
('Lobster Thermidor',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A creamy, cheesy mixture of cooked lobster meat, egg yolks, and brandy or sherry, stuffed into a lobster shell, and optionally served with an oven-browned cheese crust.','noun','Named after the play Thermidor (1891), itself named for the eleventh month of the French Republican Calendar.',109,'80_120',1,0.54,176),
('docious',NULL,'obscure',-0.38,'plain','["adjective"]','Docile, amenable to order.','adjective','Related to docity. Ultimately from Latin doceō (“to teach”); see -ous.',70,'40_80',1,0.45,177),
('tourmaline','/ˈtʊɚ.mə.lɪn/','uncommon',2.54,'plain','["noun"]','A complex black or dark-coloured borosilicate mineral, compounded with various chemical elements and considered a semi-precious stone.','noun','From French tourmaline, ultimately from Sinhalese තෝරමල්ලී (tōramallī, “carnelian”)',83,'80_120',1,0.6,178),
('Christmas tree formation',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A formation consisting of four defenders, three central midfielders, two attacking midfielders, and one striker.','noun','An overhead diagram of the formation has a pointed shape, like a Christmas tree.',80,'80_120',0,0.45,179),
('-aboo',NULL,'scarce',1.34,'hyphenated','["suffix"]','A suffix combined with a noun to create a word for a person with an intense and/or unhealthy fixation on the referent of the noun.','suffix','From weeaboo, a nonce word invented by Nicholas Gurewitch in a Perry Bible Fellowship comic, later arbitrarily used by the 4chan website as an automated replacement for wapanese in users'' posts.',194,'120_200',0,0.6,180),
('Themistius','/θɛˈmɪstɪəs/','scarce',1.1,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A male given name from Ancient Greek — famously held by','proper noun','From the Latin Themistius, from the Ancient Greek Θεμίστιος (Themístios).',73,'40_80',1,0.5,181),
('adunation',NULL,'obscure',-0.28,'plain','["noun"]','The act of uniting; union.','noun','From Latin adūnātiō, from ad + ūnus (“one”).',44,'40_80',1,0.45,182),
('Dresden','/ˈdɹɛzdən/','common',3.25,'capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','A variety of china, originally manufactured in the city, but manufactured in Meissen from the 18th century.','noun','Borrowed from German Dresden (“Dresden”) in the 1730s, from Middle High German Dresden, from a Sorbian source akin to Lower Sorbian Drježdźany, ultimately from Proto-Slavic *dręzga, of which the locative case is *dręzdzě.',221,'200plus',1,0.8,183),
('emanate','/ˈɛm.əˌneɪt/','common',3.69,'plain','["verb"]','To come from a source; issue from.','verb','From Latin ēmānāre (“to flow out, spring out of, arise, proceed from”), from ē (“out”) + mānāre (“to flow”).',108,'80_120',1,0.6,184),
('throat','/ˈθɹoʊt/','common',4.44,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','The front part of the neck.','noun','From Middle English throte, from Old English þrote, þrota, þrotu (“throat”), from Proto-West Germanic *þrotu, from Proto-Germanic *þrutō (“throat”), from Proto-Indo-European *trud- (“to swell, become stiff”). Cognate with Dutch strot (“throat”), German Drossel (“throttle, gorge of game (wild animals)”), Faroese troti (“swelling”), Icelandic þroti (“swelling”), Norwegian trut (“mouth”), Swedish trut.',402,'200plus',1,0.8,185),
('Achenbach syndrome',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','paroxysmal hand hematoma.','noun','Named after German internist Walter Achenbach, who published a description of it in the 1950s.',94,'80_120',1,0.54,186),
('rooned',NULL,'rare',0.05,'plain','["adjective"]','Ruined; utterly spoiled or destroyed.','adjective','From "we''ll all be rooned" (i.e. ruined), the refrain of a pessimistic character in John O''Brien''s 1921 poem Said Hanrahan.',123,'120_200',1,0.6,187),
('laniary',NULL,'obscure',-0.26,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A canine tooth.','noun','From Latin laniarium, from lanius (“a butcher”).',48,'40_80',1,0.45,188),
('Marianismo',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["noun"]','The concept, found in Latin American folk culture, that a virtuous woman should emulate the Virgin Mary in piousness, sexual purity, modesty, etc.','noun','Spanish marianismo',18,'lt40',1,0.3,189),
('Yug',NULL,'scarce',1.88,'capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','A Yeniseian people of central Siberia.','noun','Learned borrowing from Ket Юг (Jùk ~ Jūk, “Yug person (term used for men)”), via Russian. Yug people''s endonym was Yug Дьуʼк (Dʲuˀk).',133,'120_200',1,0.69,190),
