('Harbottle',NULL,'scarce',1.76,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A village and civil parish in central Northumberland, England (OS grid ref NT9304).','proper noun','From Old English hȳra (“hireling”) or hȳr (“hire, wages”) + bōtl (“dwelling house”).',84,'80_120',1,0.55,192),
('Ynglism',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','An esoteric Rodnover (Slavic Neopagan) sect from Russia.','proper noun','Borrowed from Russian инглиизм (ingliizm). May be reanalyzed as Yngling (“a Scandinavian royal dynasty”) + -ism, in analogy with the Russian word.
In Ynglist belief, the name refers to a fiery primordial radiance known as the "Ynglia" emanated by the supreme god Ramkha.',270,'200plus',1,0.74,193),
('cypress','/ˈsaɪ.pɹəs/','common',3.29,'plain','["noun"]','An evergreen coniferous tree with flattened shoots bearing small scale-like leaves, whose dark foliage is sometimes associated with mourning, in family Cupressaceae, especially the genera Cupressus and Chamaecyparis.','noun','From Middle English cipres, from Old French cipres (French cyprès), from Late Latin cypressus; compare Hebrew גֹּפֶר (gōp̄er), the name of the tree whose wood was used to make the ark (Genesis. vi:14), also κύπρος (relating to aromatic pitch).',243,'200plus',1,0.8,194),
('sevruga',NULL,'rare',0.93,'plain','["noun"]','stellate sturgeon (Acipenser stellatus).','noun','From Russian севрю́га (sevrjúga).',33,'lt40',1,0.35,195),
('utsuge',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["noun"]','A Japanese visual novel genre characterized by tragic plots and pervasively bleak tones.','noun','Borrowed from Japanese 鬱ゲー (utsuge, “depressing game”), a combination of 鬱 (utsu, “melancholy, gloom”) and ゲー (gē), a shortening of ゲーム (gēmu, “game”), itself from English game.',177,'120_200',1,0.69,196),
('Yao',NULL,'common',3.15,'capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','A Bantu people living in Malawi, Mozambique and Tanzania in Africa.','noun','From various romanizations of the Mandarin and Hokkien pronunciations of Chinese 姚 (Yáo/Iâu). Doublet of Yiu.',109,'80_120',1,0.69,197),
('baculine',NULL,'obscure',-0.91,'plain','["adjective"]','Of or pertaining to a rod or cane, or punishment with either of them.','adjective','Latin baculum (“staff”).',24,'lt40',1,0.35,198),
('Maryada',NULL,'scarce',1.27,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A female given name from Sanskrit used in India.','proper noun','Borrowed from Sanskrit मर्यादा (maryādā, “dignity”).',52,'40_80',1,0.49,199),
('Gewürztraminer','[ɡəˈvɝtstɹəˌmiːnɚ]','scarce',1.4,'capitalized,nonascii','["noun"]','An aromatic white wine grape variety that grows best in cooler climates.','noun','Borrowed from German Gewürztraminer, from Gewürz (“spice”) + Traminer that is from "Tramin an der Weinstraße" in South Tyrol.',125,'120_200',1,0.69,200);
