('forworship',NULL,'obscure',-0.7,'plain','["verb"]','To worship wrongly, misworship.','verb','From for- (“wrongly, amiss”) + worship.',39,'lt40',1,0.3,204),
('double-jointed','/ˌdʌbəlˈd͡ʒɔɪntɪd/','rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["adjective"]','Having one or more joints (usually those of the fingers, arms, or legs) able to bend more than is normal; possessing hypermobility of one or more joints.','adjective','PIE word
 *dwóh₁
From double (adjective) + jointed (adjective), from the fact that a body part of a person with the condition appears to have two joints bending in different directions.',185,'120_200',0,0.6,205),
('monorotor',NULL,'obscure',-0.5,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A pump or rotorcraft with a single rotor or single main rotor.','noun','From mono- + rotor. Late 19th to early 20th century, originally with reference to pumps.',88,'80_120',0,0.54,206),
('cavernous','/ˈkæv.ɚ.nəs/','common',3.09,'plain','["adjective"]','Resembling a cavern in size, shape, or atmosphere.','adjective','Late Middle English cavernous, cavernose, borrowing from Old French caverneux or Latin cavernōsus (“full of hollows or cavities”), from caverna (“a hollow, cavity, cave”) + -ōsus (“-ous, -ose”, adjectival suffix); equivalent to cavern + -ous.',242,'200plus',1,0.8,207),
('Refsum''s disease',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["noun"]','A peroxisomal neurological disease that results in the malformation of myelin sheaths around nerve cells.','noun','Named after Sigvald Bernhard Refsum (1907–1991), Norwegian neurologist .',72,'40_80',1,0.44,208),
('mazedly',NULL,'obscure',-0.93,'plain','["adverb"]','In an astonished or bewildered manner.','adverb','From Middle English masedliche, masedly; equivalent to mazed + -ly.',67,'40_80',1,0.45,209),
('dial','/ˈdaɪ.əl/','common',4.0,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A graduated, circular scale over which a needle moves to show a measurement (such as speed).','noun','The original meaning was ''sundial'' and/or ''clock dial''; from Middle English diall, from Middle French dyal, from Latin diālis (“daily, concerning the day”), because of its use in telling the time of day, from Latin diēs (“day”). Compare Spanish dial and día (“day”).',266,'200plus',1,0.8,210),
('Balazs',NULL,'scarce',1.88,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Hungarian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Hungarian Balázs.',31,'lt40',1,0.39,211),
('healful',NULL,'marginal',-1.37,'plain','["adjective"]','Tending or serving to heal; health-promoting; healing.','adjective','From Middle English heeleful, heleful, equivalent to heal (“health, well-being”) + -ful. Compare healless.',106,'80_120',1,0.5,212),
('Carnegie','/ˈkɑɹnəɡi/','common',3.61,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','Possibly from Gaelic cathair an eige, meaning "fort at the gap".',64,'40_80',1,0.45,213),
('explication',NULL,'common',3.01,'plain','["noun"]','The act of opening or unfolding.','noun','Borrowed from Middle French explication and its etymon Latin explicātiō, explicātiōnem. By surface analysis, explicate + -ion.',126,'120_200',1,0.79,214),
('digitigrade','/ˈdɪd͡ʒətəɡɹeɪd/','scarce',1.3,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A digitigrade animal; an animal that walks on its toes, such as a cat or a dog.','noun','From French digitigrade, from Latin digitus (“finger, toe, digit”) + -grade (“gait”); compare New Latin digitigradus.',117,'80_120',1,0.65,215),
('intuse',NULL,'obscure',-0.34,'plain','["noun"]','A bruise; a contusion.','noun','From Latin intundere (“to bruise”), from in- (“in”) + tundere, tusum (“to beat, bruise”).',89,'80_120',1,0.55,216),
('Ohtahara syndrome',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A progressive condition with a combination of severe epilepsy and cerebral palsy. It is characterised by severe physical and mental retardation and partial seizures.','noun','Named for the Japanese neurologist Shunsuke Ohtahara (1930–2013), who identified it in 1976.',92,'80_120',1,0.5,217),
('King''s English','/kɪŋz‿ˈɪŋ(ɡ)lɪʃ/','rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','Often preceded by the: spoken or written English regarded as used and safeguarded by the King of England; standard English characterized by correct grammar and what is thought of as proper usage of words and expressions, and (when spoken) formal British pronunciation.','proper noun','From King + -’s + English, possibly modelled after king''s coin. See the quotation from 1553.',92,'80_120',1,0.55,218),
('mankind','/mænˈkaɪnd/','common',3.99,'plain','["noun"]','The human race in its entirety.','noun','From Middle English mankynde, alteration (due to kynde = “kind, nature, sort”) of earlier mankyn, from Old English mancynn. Equivalent to man + kin, and/or man + -kind. Cognate with Scots mankind, Middle High German mankünne, Danish mandkøn, Icelandic mannkyn (“mankind”). See also mankin.',289,'200plus',1,0.8,219),
('Oppel-Kundt illusion',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,hyphenated','["noun"]','An optical illusion that occurs when comparing the sizes of filled and unfilled parts of an image.','noun','Named after German physicists Johann Joseph Oppel and August Kundt.',67,'40_80',1,0.44,220),
('forcené','/ˈfoɹ.səˌneɪ/','obscure',-0.41,'nonascii','["adjective"]','Rearing on the hind legs. (When another animal has this posture, it is termed rampant.)','adjective','From French forcené (“rabid”), past participle of forcener (“to go mad, become enraged”), from Middle French, from Old French forsener (“to be mad with rage”) (compare Old French forsenede (“one who has lost his mind”)), from for- + sen (“sense, reason, mind”), from Frankish *sinn (“sense, mind, judgement”).
Cognate with German Sinn (“sense, meaning, mind”), Dutch zin (“sense, desire”). More at for-, sense.',410,'200plus',1,0.8,221),
('pagi',NULL,'scarce',1.86,'plain','["noun"]','A native tracker; a person employed to track thieves by their footprints etc.','noun','Hindi [Term?]',13,'lt40',1,0.35,222),
('Tar Heel',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','A person associated with or supportive of the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill.','noun','North Carolina was associated with tar (of which it was a major producer) from the 1700s onwards, and residents were (at first derisively) called "Tarboilers" since at least the 1840s; "Tar Heel" is first attested in 1863 in comments by Confederate soldiers which suggest it was already in common use at that time. One popular theory suggests it refers to North Carolinians having tar on their heels to make them "stick", referring either to their reluctance to join the Confederacy, or to their holding ground during battles when other states'' troops retreated.',562,'200plus',1,0.6,223),
('Wilhelmsen',NULL,'scarce',1.5,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','Borrowed from Danish and Norwegian Wilhelmsen.',46,'40_80',1,0.54,224),
('Spaniard''s Bay','/ˈspæn.jɚdz ˈbeɪ/','rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','A town in Newfoundland, Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada.','proper noun','The town is named after a nearby bay, which is named in turn for the presence of Spanish, Basque and Portuguese fishermen around it.',132,'120_200',1,0.79,225),
('-icle',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'hyphenated','["suffix"]','Used to form diminutive nouns.','suffix','From a back-formation of icicle, the second element of which ultimately from Old English ġicel (“icicle, ice”). More at icicle, ickle.',134,'120_200',1,0.6,226),
('Kikombe cha Umoja',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','Any cup used for pouring libations to honor ancestors and communal drinking at dinner during the Kwanzaa holiday','noun','Borrowed from Swahili Kikombe cha Umoja.',40,'40_80',1,0.44,227),
