('Anjanā',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized,nonascii','["proper noun"]','The mother of Lord Hanuman and wife of Kesari, also know as Anjani.','proper noun','Borrowed from Sanskrit अञ्जना (añjanā).',39,'lt40',1,0.39,282),
('quantum','/ˈkwɑntəm/','common',4.31,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','The total amount of something; quantity.','noun','From Late Latin quantum, noun use of neuter form of Latin quantus (“how much”).',79,'40_80',1,0.5,283),
('Tacitus','/ˈtæsɪtʊs/','uncommon',2.64,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A Roman cognomen, notably borne by Publius (or Gaius) Cornelius Tacitus (c.56–117), a historian of ancient Rome and Marcus Claudius Tacitus (c.200–275), a Roman emperor.','proper noun','Latin Tacitus.',14,'lt40',1,0.4,284),
('ettling',NULL,'rare',0.94,'plain','["noun"]','The act or process of intending; intention; purpose; endeavour.','noun','From Middle English etlyng, atlyng, equivalent to ettle + -ing.',63,'40_80',1,0.45,285),
('in-',NULL,'common',7.27,'hyphenated','["prefix"]','in, into, towards, within.','prefix','From Middle English in-, borrowed (in words of Latinate origin) from Latin in-, from Latin in, from Proto-Indo-European *en (cognate to Germanic in-, above). Often borrowed from French in- (e.g. incise, incite, incline, indication), or as French en-, originally from Latin in.',276,'200plus',1,0.8,286),
('Murrow',NULL,'uncommon',2.42,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','From Old English mōr, from Proto-West Germanic *mōr (“moor”) + Middle English rewe, rowe, rawe, from Old English rǣw, rāw.',122,'120_200',1,0.8,287),
('Kleistian jar',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,pointer_only','["noun"]','Synonym of Leyden jar.','noun','Named after German cleric Ewald Georg von Kleist, who independently discovered it in 1745.',90,'80_120',1,0.54,288),
('court of cassation',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','In several legal systems, the highest-level court of appeal in procedural matters, which is empowered to quash a verdict but then refers the case back for a new trial at the previous level.','noun','Calque of French cour de cassation, from cour (“court”) + de (“of”) + cassation, from the verb casser (“to break”), indicating the power of such a court to quash the ruling of the court below.',192,'120_200',1,0.65,289),
