('corsetier',NULL,'obscure',-0.17,'plain','["noun"]','A manufacturer of corsets.','noun','From French corsetier. By surface analysis, corset + -ier.',58,'40_80',1,0.45,294),
('mighty','/ˈmaɪti/','common',4.1,'plain','["noun", "adjective", "adverb"]','Very strong; possessing might.','adjective','From Middle English mighty, mightie, mighti, myghty, miȝty, maȝty, from Old English mihtiġ, mehtiġ, meahtiġ, mæhtiġ (“mighty”), from Proto-West Germanic *mahtīg (“mighty”), from Proto-Germanic *mahtīgaz (“mighty”), equivalent to might + -y.
Cognate with Scots michty, mychty, Saterland Frisian machtich, Dutch machtig, German Low German machtig, German mächtig, Swedish mäktig.',377,'200plus',1,0.8,295),
('ondes martenot',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','An early electric keyboard instrument.','noun','From French ondes Martenot (literally “Martenot waves”), named after Maurice Martenot (1898–1980).',98,'80_120',1,0.63,296),
('rebut','/ɹɪˈbʌt/','common',3.25,'plain','["verb"]','To drive back or beat back; to repulse.','verb','Inherited from Middle English rebutten, rebouten, from Old French reboter, rebuter, rebouter, etc., from re- + boter, buter, bouter (“to butt”). Entered English around 1302-1307.',178,'120_200',1,0.8,297),
('pro bono','/proʊ ˈboʊnoʊ/','rare',NULL,'multiword','["prepositional phrase"]','Without charging the usual fee; done voluntarily without fee, generally for clients or patients of limited means.','prepositional phrase','Abbreviated from Latin prō bonō publicō (“for the public good”).',64,'40_80',1,0.45,298),
