('sploshing',NULL,'scarce',1.09,'plain','["noun"]','A splosh, or the sounds of a splosh.','noun','In sense “wet and messy fetish”, coined or popularized by 1989 British magazine Splosh!',87,'80_120',1,0.54,301),
('Tisha B''Av',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','An annual day of fasting, associated with a number of disasters in Jewish history.','proper noun','From Hebrew תִּשְׁעָה בְּאָב (Tish''a B''Áv, “the ninth day of Av”), from תִּשְׁעָה (tish''á, “nine”) + בְּ (b''-, “in”) + אָב (''Áv, “Av, fifth month of the Jewish Calendar”).',171,'120_200',1,0.6,302),
('rijsttafel','/ˈɹaɪsˌtɑːfəl/','rare',0.94,'plain','["noun"]','A large meal originating in colonial Indonesia composed of many different dishes with rice as the main ingredient; several side-dishes such as krupuk, acar and serundeng are served.','noun','Borrowed from Dutch rijsttafel (“rice table”).',46,'40_80',1,0.49,303),
('draw a blank',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["verb"]','To be unable to recall a required piece of information.','verb','Probably from the idea of drawing of a blank (losing) ticket in an old-fashioned lottery.',89,'80_120',0,0.5,304),
('Baraita',NULL,'scarce',1.04,'capitalized','["noun"]','Any of various Jewish traditions that are not included in the Mishnah.','noun','Aramaic baraita, an extraneous teaching. The word appears in the feminine (baraita rather than bara''ah), because it is actually short for matnita baraita, external Mishnah or teaching (and matnita is feminine).',210,'200plus',1,0.65,305),
('Kohnstamm''s phenomenon',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','The sustained involuntary contraction of a muscle after a prolonged voluntary contraction.','proper noun','First described by German neurologist Oskar Kohnstamm in 1915.',62,'40_80',1,0.4,306),
('birthhouse',NULL,'obscure',-0.26,'plain','["noun"]','A building used for birthing.','noun','From birth + house. Cognate with West Frisian bertehûs, Dutch geboortehuis, German Geburtshaus.',95,'80_120',1,0.69,307),
('cannelloni',NULL,'scarce',1.72,'plain','["noun"]','Wide tubes of pasta filled with a savoury stuffing and baked in the oven.','noun','From Italian cannelloni, from Latin canna (“reed”).',51,'40_80',1,0.5,308),
('pteron',NULL,'scarce',1.0,'plain','["noun"]','A peristyle raised on a podium, differing from an ordinary peristyle raised only on a stylobate.','noun','From Ancient Greek πτερόν (pterón, “feather, wing”).',52,'40_80',1,0.45,309),
('swap','/ˈswɑp/','common',3.94,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','An exchange of two comparable things.','noun','From Middle English swappen (“to swap”), originally meaning "to hurl" or "to strike", the word alludes to striking hands together when making an exchange; probably from Old English *swappian, a secondary form of Old English swāpan (“to swoop”). Cognate with German schwappen (“to slosh, slop”). Compare also Middle English swippen (“to strike, hit”), from Old English swipian (“to scourge, strike, beat, lash”), Old Norse svipa (“to swoop, flash, whip, look after, look around”). More at swipe.',494,'200plus',1,0.8,310),
('gesture','/ˈd͡ʒɛs.t͡ʃɚ/','common',4.37,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A motion of the limbs or body, especially one made to emphasize speech.','noun','From Medieval Latin gestūra (“a mode of action”), from Latin gerō (“to bear; (reflexive) bear oneself, behave, act”), past participle gestus. By surface analysis, Latin gest- + -ure.',182,'120_200',1,0.75,311),
('sock','/sɑk/','common',4.03,'plain','["noun", "verb", "adjective", "interjection"]','A knitted or woven covering for the foot.','noun','From Middle English socke, sokke, sok, from Old English socc (“sock, light shoe, slipper”), a West Germanic borrowing from Latin soccus (“a light shoe or slipper, buskin”), from Ancient Greek σύκχος (súkkhos, “a kind of shoe”), probably from Phrygian or from an Anatolian language. Beekes compares Avestan 𐬵𐬀𐬑𐬀 (haxa, “sole of the foot”). Cognate with Scots sok (“sock, stocking”), West Frisian sok (“sock”), Dutch sok (“sock”), German Socke (“sock”), Danish sok, sokke (“sock”), Swedish sock, socka (“sock”), Icelandic sokkur (“sock”). Doublet of zocco; also related to zoccolo, socle, and zocalo.',598,'200plus',1,0.8,312),
('trumpet','/ˈtɹʌmpɪt/','common',3.73,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A musical instrument of the brass family, generally tuned to the key of B-flat; by extension, any type of lip-vibrated aerophone, most often valveless and not chromatic.','noun','From Middle English trumpet, trumpette, trompette (“trumpet”), from Old French trompette (“trumpet”), diminutive of trompe (“horn, trump, trumpet”), from Frankish *trumpa, *trumba (“trumpet”), ultimately imitative.
Cognate with Old High German trumpa, trumba (“horn, trumpet”), Middle Dutch tromme (“drum”), Middle Low German trumme (“drum”), Old Norse trumba (“pipe; trumpet”). More at drum.
Displaced native English beme, from Middle English beme, from Old English bīeme.',473,'200plus',1,0.8,313),
('casuistics',NULL,'rare',0.42,'plain','["noun"]','The recording and study of individual cases.','noun','From casuistic + -s, equivalent to Latin casus + -istics, in the medical sense under the influence of English case. Also by surface analysis, casu(al) + -istics or by surface analysis, casu(al) + -ist + -ics or by surface analysis, casuist + -ics.',247,'200plus',1,0.7,314),
