('Ogg','/ɒɡ/','uncommon',2.47,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','An open bitstream container file format for audio, video, and other multimedia content.','proper noun','An abbreviation of OggSquish, a 1993 suggestion by Michael Whitsun to replace the original name (Squish) which was trademarked. Ogg was jargon from the computer game Netrek meaning roughly: to put all your resources into an attack to ensure its success, regardless of any negative consequences on the rest of the game. Allegedly ogg is a corruption of Og, the screen-name of the player who first played the game this way.',421,'200plus',0,0.7,390),
('God Emperor',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','A god-king; a sovereign worshiped as a god.','noun','Its use in reference to the Emperor of Japan is a calque of Japanese 天皇 (tennō, literally “heavenly sovereign/emperor”), referring to the emperor''s descent from the sun goddess Amaterasu.
In reference to Donald Trump, from Trump-supporting Internet users humorously depicting him as the God-Emperor of Mankind, a character from the British science fiction tabletop game Warhammer 40,000.',387,'200plus',1,0.74,391),
('fortitude','/ˈfoɹ.tɪˌtud/','common',3.15,'plain','["noun"]','Mental or emotional strength that enables courage in the face of adversity.','noun','From Middle English fortitude, from Old French, from Latin fortitūdō (“bravery, strength”), from fortis (“brave, strong”).',122,'120_200',1,0.8,392),
('confrere',NULL,'uncommon',2.03,'plain','["noun"]','A colleague or fellow, especially a professional one.','noun','From Old French confrere, from Latin confrāter.
The word which was originally only used for the second sense became obsolete in the 17th century and reappeared in the mid 18th century as a re-borrowing of Modern French confrère whence the first sense is from.',259,'200plus',1,0.8,393),
('Wenke',NULL,'scarce',1.34,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from German.','proper noun','Borrowed from German Wenke.',27,'lt40',1,0.39,394),
('Zaveri',NULL,'scarce',1.31,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Gujarati.','proper noun','Borrowed from Gujarati ઝવેરી (jhaverī, “jeweler”),
from Persian جواهری (javâheri, “jeweller”),
borrowed from Arabic جَوَاهِر (jawāhir),
ultimately borrowed from Early Classical Persian گوهر (gawhar, “jewel, gem, pearl, essence”)',228,'200plus',1,0.79,395),
('Drayton','/ˈdɹeɪ.tən/','uncommon',2.84,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A suburban area of Portsmouth, Hampshire, England (OS grid ref SU672860).','proper noun','From Middle English Drayton, from Old English Drægtūn (literally “town where logs are dragged”), from draġan (“to draw, drag”) + tūn (“enclosure, town”). Alternatively from Proto-Brythonic *treβ (“town, settlement”) + Old English tūn.',234,'200plus',1,0.8,396),
('Yerevan','/ˌjeɹəˈvɑːn/','uncommon',2.7,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','The capital and largest city of Armenia.','proper noun','Borrowed from Armenian Երեւան (Erewan).',39,'lt40',1,0.44,397),
('trich-',NULL,'scarce',1.63,'hyphenated,pointer_only','["prefix"]','Alternative form of tricho- (“hair”).','prefix','From Latinized form of Ancient Greek τρῐχός (trĭkhós, “hair”).',62,'40_80',1,0.45,398),
('stoic','/ˈstoʊ.ɪk/','common',3.44,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','Proponent of stoicism, a school of thought, from in 300 B.C.E. up to about the time of Marcus Aurelius, who holds that by cultivating an understanding of the logos, or natural law, one can be free of suffering.','noun','From Latin stōicus (noun via Middle English Stoycis pl), from Ancient Greek Στωϊκός (Stōïkós), from Ποικίλη Στοά (Poikílē Stoá, “the Stoa Poikile”, literally “painted portico”), the portico in Athens where Zeno of Citium was teaching.',234,'200plus',1,0.8,399),
('Parascandola',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Italian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Parascandola.',35,'lt40',1,0.34,400);
INSERT INTO word (word,ipa,tier,zipf,shape,pos,definition,def_pos,etymology,etym_len,etym_band,has_signal,prior,shuffle) VALUES
('sweet fifteen',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,pointer_only','["noun"]','Synonym of quinceanera.','noun','From sweet + fifteen. Coined in analogy to sweet sixteen; Americanization of the Mexican concept of quinceaños or quinceañera.',126,'120_200',0,0.59,401),
('jugata',NULL,'obscure',-0.35,'plain','["adjective"]','Side by side or joined.','adjective','Latin, neuter plural of iugatus (“joined”).',43,'40_80',1,0.45,402),
('ikaite',NULL,'rare',0.68,'plain','["noun"]','An evaporite, consisting of hydrated calcium carbonate with the chemical formula CaCO₃·6H₂O, found in cold marine waters.','noun','Named after Ikka Fjord (formerly spelled Ika) a small fjord in southwestern Greenland.',86,'80_120',0,0.54,403),
('Llanfyllin','/ɬænˈvʌɬɪn/','scarce',1.36,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A small town and community with a town council in Powys, Wales; historically in Montgomeryshire (OS grid ref SJ1419).','proper noun','From Welsh.',11,'lt40',1,0.35,404),
('bland','/blænd/','common',3.5,'plain','["noun", "verb", "adjective"]','Mixture; union.','noun','From Middle English bland, from Old English bland, blond (“blending, mixture, confusion”), from Proto-Germanic *blandą (“a mixing, mixture”), from Proto-Indo-European *bʰlendʰ- (“to grow turbid, dim, see badly, be blind”). Cognate with Icelandic blanda (“a mixture of liquids, especially of hot whey and water”).',312,'200plus',1,0.8,405),
('Nauert',NULL,'scarce',1.4,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from German.','proper noun','Borrowed from German Nauert.',28,'lt40',1,0.39,406),
('Qanungo',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','Transliteration of Punjabi ਕ਼ਾਨੂੰਗੋ (qānūṅgo) / قانُونگو (qānūngo), Urdu قَانُون گو (qānūn go) / Hindi क़ानूनगो (qānūngo), Sindhi قانُون گو (qānū̃ go) / क़ानूंगो / 𑊺𑋩𑋠𑋑𑋤𑋟𑊼𑋧 (qānūṅgo), Gujarati કાનૂગો (kānūgo), Marwari कानूंगौ (kānūṅgau) / 𑅕𑅧𑅒𑅧𑅗𑅒 (knungu), Maithili कानूनगो (kānūnᵊgō) / 𑒏𑒰𑒢𑒴𑒢𑒑𑒼 (kānūnagō), Odia କାନୁନ୍ଗୋ (kānungo), Bengali কানুনগো (kanungō), Assamese কাননগো (kanongü), from Classical Persian قانون گو (qānūn-go). This designation was used in South Asia for hereditary registrar of landed property in a subdivision of a district, meaning “an expounder of law”.',575,'200plus',1,0.8,407),
('breunnerite',NULL,'rare',0.08,'plain','["noun"]','A type of magnesite with a magnesium/iron ratio of 90/10 to 70/30.','noun','Named in honour of Count August Breunner (or Breuner; 1796-1877), a mineral collector and a government official of Austria-Hungary.',131,'120_200',0,0.6,408),
('loof',NULL,'scarce',1.72,'plain','["noun"]','The palm of the hand.','noun','Inherited from Middle English lof (“a contrivance for altering a ship''s course, paddle, oar”), from Middle Dutch loef (“an oar or paddle used in steering”), ultimately of the same origin as Etymology 1. Compare luff.',216,'200plus',1,0.7,409),
('Dimidium',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','51 Pegasi b, a large hot Jupiter exoplanet, approximately 50 light-years away from Earth in the constellation of Pegasus.','proper noun','Borrowed from Latin dīmidium (“half”), referring to the planet''s mass of approximately half the mass of Jupiter.',112,'80_120',1,0.54,410),
('Rider',NULL,'common',4.02,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname originating as an occupation. More often spelled Ryder.','proper noun','* As an English surname, from the noun rider.
* Also as an English surname, from Old English rēodan (“to clear out”), from Proto-West Germanic *reudan. Compare Reeder.
* Also as an English surname, from dialectal rithe (“small stream”).
* As an Irish surname, calque from Irish ó marcaigh (“descendant of the (horse)rider”). This is also found directly in Markey.
* As a German surname, Americanized from Reiter.',412,'200plus',1,0.8,411),
('controller','/kənˈtɹoʊlɚ/','common',4.24,'plain','["noun"]','One who controls something.','noun','From Middle English countreroller, from Anglo-Norman contreroulour and Middle French contreroleur (French contrôleur), from Medieval Latin contrārotulātor, from *contrārotulāre (from which control). Doublet of comptroller. By surface analysis, control + -er.',258,'200plus',1,0.8,412),
('cheletropic',NULL,'rare',0.53,'plain','["adjective"]','Describing a pericyclic addition reaction across the terminal atoms of a fully conjugated system in which two new single bonds are formed with a single atom of the reactant.','adjective','From Ancient Greek χηλή (khēlḗ) + + -tropic.',44,'40_80',1,0.45,413),
('avital','/əˈvɪtəl/','scarce',1.79,'plain','["adjective"]','Relating to a grandfather or ancestor.','adjective','From Latin avus (“grandfather”).',32,'lt40',1,0.35,414),
('Shevardnadze',NULL,'uncommon',2.12,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A transliteration of the Georgian surname შევარდნაძე (ševardnaʒe).','proper noun','From Georgian შევარდნაძე (ševardnaʒe).',38,'lt40',1,0.4,415),
('Memphitical',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized,pointer_only','["adjective"]','Synonym of Memphitic.','adjective','From Latin Memphīticus + -al. By surface analysis, Memphite + -ical.',68,'40_80',1,0.4,416),
('glacious',NULL,'obscure',-0.97,'plain','["adjective"]','Characteristic, composed, or of the nature of icy; icy.','adjective','From obsolete French glacieux, from Latin glaciēs (“ice”).',58,'40_80',1,0.5,417),
('lectionary',NULL,'uncommon',2.44,'plain','["noun"]','A book or listing that contains a collection of scripture readings appointed for Christian or Jewish worship on a given day or occasion.','noun','Learned borrowing from Ecclesiastical Latin lēctiōnārium.',57,'40_80',1,0.54,418),
('temperance','/ˈtɛmpəɹəns/','common',3.34,'plain','["noun"]','Habitual moderation in regard to the indulgence of the natural appetites and passions; restrained or moderate indulgence.','noun','From Anglo-Norman temperance, from Latin temperantia (“moderation, sobriety, discretion, self-control”), from temperans, present participle of temperare (“to moderate”). See temper. English equivalent temper + -ance.',216,'200plus',1,0.8,419),
('history of the four kings',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A pack of cards.','noun','Said to have originated in France, where the kings in a pack of cards once bore the names of four real historical kings.',120,'120_200',0,0.55,420),
('tessella',NULL,'rare',0.37,'plain','["noun"]','A small tessera.','noun','From Latin tessella.',20,'lt40',1,0.35,421),
('Odaenathus',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','Septimius Odaenathus, the first king of the Palmyrene kingdom, who reigned from A.D. 260 to A. D. 267','proper noun','From Latin Odaenathus.',22,'lt40',1,0.35,422),
('perhalogenation',NULL,'obscure',-0.95,'plain','["noun"]','The act or process of halogenating.','noun','By surface analysis, perhalogenate + -ion, or, by surface analysis, per- + halogenation.',88,'80_120',0,0.5,423),
('khubzism',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["noun"]','The repression of political parties, human rights, and the press in Tunisia under President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali.','noun','From Arabic خُبْز (ḵubz, “bread”) + -ism, in the idea of bread and circuses.',76,'40_80',1,0.4,424),
('podoscaph',NULL,'marginal',-1.57,'plain','["noun"]','Each of a pair of ski-shaped canoe-like floats attached to the feet, used for walking on water.','noun','From podo- + Ancient Greek σκάφη (skáphē, “light boat”).',56,'40_80',1,0.4,425),
('German','/ˈd͡ʒɝ.mən/','common',4.95,'capitalized','["noun", "adjective", "proper noun"]','A native or inhabitant of Germany; a person of German citizenship or nationality.','noun','From Latin Germānus, Germānī (“the peoples of Germānia”), as distinct from Gauls (in the writings of Caesar and Tacitus), and of uncertain ultimate origin (possibly Celtic/Gaulish).
Not related to german (“closely related”) or germane (from the Latin adjective germānus, through Old French).
Attested since at least 1520. Replaced the older terms Almain and Dutch (from Proto-Germanic *þiudiskaz) in English. Besides cognates of German, Almain, and Dutch, two other categories of words for the Germans in other languages are cognates of Saxon and descendants of Proto-Slavic *němьcь; see those entries for more.
The surname is generally from the noun, though sometimes confused with Herman, Hermann under Russian influence. As a German surname, Americanized from Germann. Compare Germán, Germain, Jerman.',804,'200plus',1,0.8,426),
('ballerino','/ˌbæləˈɹiːnoʊ/','scarce',1.69,'plain','["noun"]','A male ballet dancer.','noun','Borrowed from Italian ballerino.',32,'lt40',1,0.39,427),
('rightthink',NULL,'marginal',-1.57,'pointer_only','["noun"]','Synonym of goodthink.','noun','Compound of right + think, modeled after wrongthink, ultimately modeled on crimethink from George Orwell’s Nineteen Eighty-Four.',128,'120_200',0,0.55,428),
('dwang','/dwæŋ/','rare',0.48,'plain','["noun"]','A horizontal timber (or steel) section used in the construction of a building.','noun','Borrowed from Dutch dwang, from Middle Dutch dwanc.',51,'40_80',1,0.54,429),
('zingel',NULL,'rare',0.86,'plain','["noun"]','A fish of the perch family, found in the Danube, Zingel zingel.','noun','Borrowed from German Zingel.',28,'lt40',1,0.39,430),
('abolishment','/əˈbɑl.ɪʃ.mənt/','uncommon',2.35,'plain','["noun"]','The act of abolishing; abolition; destruction.','noun','From Middle French abolissement, from aboliss-, stem of some conjugated forms abolir, equivalent to abolish + -ment.',116,'80_120',1,0.6,431),
('mokele-mbembe',NULL,'rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["noun"]','A large water-dwelling cryptid described in legends of the Congo River basin.','noun','From Lingala mokɛle-mbɛmbe, possibly meaning "one who stops the flow of rivers", or "rainbow".',94,'80_120',1,0.5,432),
('appassionato',NULL,'scarce',1.42,'plain','["adverb"]','(to be played) passionately','adverb','Borrowed from Italian appassionato.',35,'lt40',1,0.39,433),
('Kalu',NULL,'uncommon',2.07,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Igbo.','proper noun','Borrowed from Igbo.',19,'lt40',1,0.44,434),
('prolific','/pɹəˈlɪf.ɪk/','common',3.6,'plain','["adjective"]','Fertile; producing offspring or fruit in abundance, applied to plants producing fruit, animals producing young, etc.','adjective','First use appears c. 1635. From French prolifique and its etymon Latin prōlificus, from prōlēs (“offspring”) + -ficus (“making”).',129,'120_200',1,0.75,435),
('Ball',NULL,'common',5.04,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','An English surname.','proper noun','Multiple theories and origins.
* From a short form of the Middle English given name Baldwin.
* A toponymic surname for a person that lives near a knoll or rounded hill (i.e. somewhere shaped like a ball).
* From the Old Norse given name Balle.
* A habitational surname for a person from Ball, Cornwall, from Cornish Pelen.',322,'200plus',1,0.8,436),
('Sommerfeld''s constant',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,apostrophe','["proper noun"]','The fine-structure constant.','proper noun','Named after German physicist Arnold Sommerfeld.',47,'40_80',1,0.44,437),
('calcitrate',NULL,'obscure',-0.71,'plain','["verb"]','To kick.','verb','From Latin calcitratus, past participle of calcitrare.',54,'40_80',1,0.45,438),
('sepurture',NULL,'marginal',NULL,'plain','["adjective"]','Of a bird''s wings: raised above the back and opened.','adjective','Obscure. Early uses are in the phrase in sepurture, suggesting it might have originated as a noun ending in -ure; compare the heraldic use of overture, overt.',158,'120_200',0,0.55,439),
('ohm','/oʊm/','common',3.19,'plain','["noun"]','In the International System of Units, the derived unit of electrical resistance; the electrical resistance of a device across which a potential difference of one volt causes a current of one ampere.','noun','Named after Bavarian physicist Georg Ohm. A German surname, first recorded in the 12th century, from German Ohm (“uncle”), from Proto-West Germanic *auhaim (“maternal uncle”), from or related to Proto-Germanic *awô (“grandfather, paternal uncle”) + *haimaz (“home”). Doublet of oom and of dialectal eam (“uncle”).',313,'200plus',1,0.8,440),
('virelai','/ˈvɪɹəleɪ/','scarce',1.29,'plain','["noun"]','A medieval poetic form consisting of two or more three line units in each stanza, with the rhyme scheme AABAAB.','noun','From Old French virelai, alteration (after lai) of vireli.',58,'40_80',1,0.45,441),
('fix someone''s wagon',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,apostrophe','["verb"]','To cause injury, distress, or inconvenience to someone, especially as punishment or as a comeuppance.','verb','From an archaic sense of fix, "to fix in place, render unable to move", which survives in the word affix.',105,'80_120',1,0.5,442),
('snake''s head',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,apostrophe','["noun"]','snake''s head fritillary (Fritillaria meleagris), a flowering plant in the family Liliaceae, native to Eurasia.','noun','From snake + -''s + head. An upright portion of each plant bears a checkered or overlapping pattern that resembles the appearance of snakeskin.',142,'120_200',0,0.6,443),
('mitumba',NULL,'rare',0.76,'plain','["noun"]','Second-hand clothing, especially that provided by aid agencies and subsequently sold.','noun','From Swahili mitumba, plural of mtumba (“bundle, bale”). Second-hand clothes are often packaged in bundles or bales.',116,'80_120',1,0.55,444),
('Hatoyama',NULL,'scarce',1.66,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Japanese.','proper noun','Borrowed from Japanese 鳩山 (Hatoyama).',37,'lt40',1,0.39,445),
('Mörön',NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized,nonascii','["proper noun"]','A city in Khövsgöl, Mongolia.','proper noun','From Mongolian Мөрөн (Mörön).',29,'lt40',1,0.35,446),
('soy boy',NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','An effeminate or unmasculine man.','noun','From the idea that men who prefer soy to dairy and meat are weak and feminine, and that the naturally occurring phytoestrogen in soy feminizes men; see Soybean § Phytochemicals on WikipediaWikipedia for more.',208,'200plus',0,0.6,447),
('Achaemenid','/əˈkiːmənɪd/','uncommon',2.01,'capitalized','["noun", "adjective"]','A member of the dynasty which ruled the Persian empire c. 550–330 BCE (the Achaemenid Empire).','noun','Borrowed from Latin Achaemenidēs, from Ancient Greek Ἀχαιμενίδης (Akhaimenídēs), from Ancient Greek Ἀχαιμένης (Akhaiménēs) + Ancient Greek -ίδης (-ídēs, patronymic suffix). By surface analysis, Achaemenes + -id.',211,'200plus',1,0.79,448),
('molendinaceous',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'plain','["adjective"]','Resembling a windmill.','adjective','From Latin molendīna, from molendīnum (“mill, mill-house”) + -aceous.',69,'40_80',1,0.4,449),
('gwop','/ɡwɑp/','obscure',-0.39,'plain','["noun"]','money, moola','noun','Uncertain. May be a dialectal pronunciation of Spanish guapo used as a noun, thus being a doublet of wop. The attestation of the aforementioned term in English as guapo in the same sense may likely be a secondary interpretation, cf. buckaroo meaning buck (“dollar”). The acronym of George Washington On Paper is likely a backronym.',331,'200plus',1,0.79,450),
('pajeet','/pəˈd͡ʒiːt/','marginal',NULL,'plain','["noun"]','A person of South Asian ancestry.','noun','An invented Indian-sounding given name, originating from the Pajeet, my son meme created on the 4chan message board /int/ in July 2015, mocking South Asians. This was itself inspired by the Mehmet, my son meme, popularized on /int/ in late 2014, which mocked Turkish people. Although the name does not exist in South Asia, similar-sounding names exist, especially in the Indian state of Punjab. It is likely derived from Punjabi ਭਾ ਜੀ (bhā jī) / بھا جی (bhā jī, “my brother”, a honorific address to men), Hindi पाजी (pājī), as well as from Punjabi male names ending with the Hindi suffix जीत (jīt, “victory”), such as Baljeet and Sanjeet.',638,'200plus',1,0.75,451),
('zygapophysis','/ˌzaɪɡəˈpɒfɪsɪs/','scarce',1.0,'plain','["noun"]','One of the articular processes of a vertebra, of which there are usually four, two superior and two inferior.','noun','Ancient Greek ζυγόν (zugón, “yoke”) + apophysis.',48,'40_80',1,0.45,452),
('Raucci',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Italian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Raucci.',29,'lt40',1,0.34,453),
('cresset','/ˈkɹɛs.ɪt/','scarce',1.84,'plain','["noun"]','A small furnace or iron cage to hold fire for charring the inside of a cask, and making the staves flexible.','noun','Inherited from Middle English cresset, from Old French crasset, cresset (“sort of lamp or torch”); perhaps of Old Dutch or Old High German origin, and akin to English cruse and/or French creuset (“crucible”), the latter being from Gallo-Roman Vulgar Latin *croceolus.',267,'200plus',1,0.8,454),
('haphephobia','/ˌhæfɪˈfəʊbiə/','obscure',-0.28,'plain','["noun"]','The fear of being touched.','noun','From Ancient Greek ἁφή (haphḗ, “touch”) from ἅπτω (háptō, “I touch”) + -phobia.',79,'40_80',1,0.45,455),
('fish storm',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A storm, particularly a hurricane or tropical storm, which turns out in the open Atlantic or Pacific and poses no threat to land.','noun','From fish + storm, from the observation that it is a storm that seemingly only impacts fish.',92,'80_120',0,0.45,456),
('kohl','/koʊl/','common',3.2,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A dark powder, usually powdered stibnite (an antimony ore), used as eye makeup, especially in Eastern countries.','noun','From Arabic كُحْل (kuḥl, “antimony; kohl, stibnite (powdered antimony)”). Doublet of alcohol and alcool.',104,'80_120',1,0.64,457),
('ramen','/ˈɹɑmən/','common',3.35,'plain','["noun", "interjection"]','Soup noodles of wheat, with various ingredients (Japanese-Chinese style).','noun','Borrowed from Japanese ラーメン (rāmen), from Mandarin 拉麵 /拉面 (lāmiàn, “pulled noodles”). Doublet of laghman, lamian, and ramyeon.',126,'120_200',1,0.8,458),
('alvar',NULL,'uncommon',2.41,'plain','["noun"]','A limestone pavement.','noun','From Swedish alvar (“alvar”), from alv (“subsoil”). Possibly related to Old High German Alp (“alpine pasture; alp”).',116,'80_120',1,0.65,459),
('bandpass','/ˈbænd.pæs/','uncommon',2.75,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A filter that lets through only a specific range of electromagnetic frequencies.','noun','From band + pass. First emerged in the context of early radio communication; attested in patents filed by Western Electric in 1919.',131,'120_200',0,0.65,460),
('Drahunov',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A masculine surname from Ukrainian','proper noun','From Ukrainian Драгунов (Drahunov).',35,'lt40',1,0.3,461),
('argenteous',NULL,'obscure',-0.85,'plain','["adjective"]','Silvery.','adjective','Borrowed from Latin argenteus or formed from Latin argentum, suffixed with -ous.',80,'80_120',1,0.59,462),
('Richard of York gave battle in vain',NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["phrase"]','A mnemonic phrase to help remember the order of the seven colours of the rainbow: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.','phrase','A reference to Richard of York, 3rd Duke of York (1411–1460), who was killed in the Battle of Wakefield.',104,'80_120',0,0.45,463),
('elutriate','/ɪˈluːtɹɪeɪt/','scarce',1.61,'plain','["verb"]','To decant; to purify something by straining it.','verb','From Latin elutriare (“wash out”), from e- + lutriare (“wash”).',63,'40_80',1,0.45,464),
('eft','/ɛft/','uncommon',2.84,'plain','["noun", "adverb"]','A newt, especially a smooth newt (Lissotriton vulgaris, syn. Triturus punctatus), of Europe.','noun','From Middle English eft, from Old English eft, æft, from Proto-West Germanic *afti, from Proto-Germanic *afti. Cognate with Old Saxon eft, aft, eht (“again, afterwards”), Middle Low German echt (“again, afterward”). Compare English after, aft.',243,'200plus',1,0.8,465),
